.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create ranged pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_r_mempool *ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    void *buffer,
    size_t buf_size,
    uint8_t smallest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t largest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two,
    ccol_r_memory_fallback_policy_t fb_policy,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a ranged memory pool from a buffer that the caller gives. It takes
no storage for a sub-pool from the heap. The library divides the buffer into
contiguous segments, one for each size tier. It uses the same progression of
sizes and counts as ccol_r_mempool_create(). This is useful for embedded
systems.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                     Pointer to preallocated buffer
buf_size                   Size of buffer in bytes (must match exactly
                          what CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER
                          produces for the same parameters)
smallest_size_power_of_two              Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
fb_policy                 Fallback policy for exhausted pools
single_threaded           If true, omit locking
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL
err                       Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created ranged memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer size must match exactly what
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER produces for the same
  parameters
ccol_r_mempool_destroy() does not free the buffer. The caller owns the life
  of that buffer
The pool structs still come from mmgmt_procs
Each sub-pool calls the functions of mmgmt_procs for its dynamic
fallback entries, and for its own bookkeeping of them, in the middle of
an update of its own state, with its lock held when it has one. Those
functions must therefore not call back into this same ranged pool: with
a lock the call can deadlock, and without one it corrupts that state. An
allocator built on a pool must draw from a different pool
The buffer holds every sub-pool in segments that sit next to each other
The function returns NULL with an error if the buffer is not aligned to at
  least 16 bytes. That is the alignment that every entry the pool hands out
  is sure to meet. Entry 0 sits at the address of the buffer itself, so the
  buffer has to meet it too. A buffer that
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER declares already does
The same power-of-two limits on size and count as ccol_r_mempool_create()
  apply here
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_destroy (3)
